# DEVLOG #01 - Game Off 2026

My initial goal this month was to partake in the Epic Megajam 2026, despite them throwing AI in all the marketing and being sponsored by mostly just AI companies. 
AI was optional and I'll be honest that prize money, very tempting. But I ran into two problems, one the last time I used Unreal Engine it was known as UE4 & two apparently nobody at 
Epic Games owns a Linux device. As part of the requirements was to upload either a Mac or Windows build (You could also do Android but I'm not trying to figure out Mobile controls), now
yes I could have whipped up an emulator for the build but my 5 year old gaming laptop is way too underpowered to make that worth it.

Fortunately itch.io is home to millions of other Game Jams, which is why I will be doing the 2026 Game Off.

These dev logs will not be a summary but 99% of the time written live, which is why as of the time of writing this first post. I honestly have no idea what game I'm building. But I know that
I want to try out the bevy engine. So I'm gonna get that set up and then probably not actually start till tomorrow. 

## Setting up bevy

Obviously the first step for setting up bevy is making a rust project. I'll be using workspaces out of habbit but it probably wont matter much unless I make a multiplayer game.
Then I can have the server & client code sit separately.

*update.. I forgot how long Rust takes to build :P*

Alright, I've got the basics down. Now toying with plugins.

## What in the license

Decided to give setting up a proper license a go, fuck me is tedious. Making an open source game is just that little bit harder then a close source one. Figured it out now though... I hope.
